  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 105 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nAt 1PM CDT, skies are mostly sunny. Some daytime heating/mixing \nstrato-cu has developed/moved north into portions of the Missouri \nRiver valley region (west river). Temperatures have been warming \nthrough the 40s across much of the region. The breezy to windy 15 to \n30 mph south-southeast winds with gusts up to 40 mph continue, as \nwell, mainly throughout and west of the James River valley.\n\nThis highly amplified and blocky Omega block in place across NOAM \n(upper ridge axis centered on this CWA) will persist through Sunday \nnight, according to consensus model guidance. The upper wave pattern \nundergoes some changes between Monday and the end of the period, \nthough, as a couple of chunks of energy move into the western CONUS \nMonday and Tuesday, and eventually over to the east coast. \nModels/qpf guidance continue to reduce the amount of precipitation \npotential with the first area of low pressure (Wednesday \nnight/Thursday system) as it works its way across the nation's mid-\nsection mid-week. Guidance is still indicating precipitation \npotential (higher probability of rain than snow), though, with the \nlate week system/upper trof working east across the CONUS. Then, the \nupper ridge is supposed to pump back up across the middle two-thirds \nof the country.\n\nBoth ensemble probabilities and deterministic thermal progs support \nwarm (warmer than November normals) temperatures through, at least, \nTuesday of next week, perhaps even Wednesday depending on the \namount/duration of cloudiness, before any low level CAA can happen.\n\nThese breezy/windy southerly winds of 15 to 30 mph with higher gusts \nare progged to continue through Monday. Sunday night, there could \neven be a moderately strong downslope wind event in the lee of the \nPrairie Coteau.",
